}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s1\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs32\cf2\lang1033 {\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 What Is a Computer?\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 A computer consists of a C}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 P}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 U, memory, hard disk, floppy disk, monitor, printer, and communication devices.\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s1\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs32\cf2\lang1033 {\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 C}{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2\cf1  }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 P}{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2\cf1  }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 U\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li403\fi-403 {\pntext\pard\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs40 \'95\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnfs40{\pntxtb \'95}}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs40 The central processing unit (C}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs40 P}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs40 U) is the brain of a computer. It retrieves instructions from memory and executes them. The unit of measurement of clock speed is the hertz (H}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs40 z), with 1 hertz equaling 1 pulse per second. In the 19}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs40 90s, computers measured clock speed in megahertz (M}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs40 H}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs40 z), but C}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs40 P}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs40 U speed has been improving continuously; the clock speed of a computer is now usually stated in gigahertz (G}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs40 H}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs40 z). Intel\'92s newest processors run at about 3 G}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs40 H}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs40 z.\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s1\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs32\cf2\lang1033 {\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 Memory\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs44 Memory}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs44  is to store data and program instructions for C}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs44 P}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs44 U to execute. A memory unit is an ordered sequence of bytes, each holds eight bits. A program and its data must be brought to memory before they can be executed. A memory byte is never empty, but its initial content may be meaningless to your program. The current content of a memory byte is lost whenever new information is placed in it.\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s1\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs32\cf2\lang1033 {\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 How Data Is Stored?\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs32 Data of various kinds, such as numbers, characters, and strings, are encoded as a series of bits (zeros and ones). Computers use zeros and ones because digital devices have two stable states, which are referred to as }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs32 zero}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs32  and }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs32 one}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs32  by convention. The programmers need not to be concerned about the encoding and decoding of data, which is performed automatically by the system based on the encoding scheme. The encoding scheme varies. For example, character \'91J\'92 is represented by 01001010 in one byte. A small number such as three can be stored in a single byte. If computer needs to store a large number that cannot fit into a single byte, it uses a number of adjacent bytes. No two data can share or split a same byte. A byte is the minimum storage unit.\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s1\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs32\cf2\lang1033 {\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 Storage Devices\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 Memory is volatile, because information is lost when the power is off. Programs and data are permanently stored on storage devices and are moved to memory when the computer actually uses them. There are three main types of storage devices: Disk drives (hard disks), C}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 D drives (C}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 D-R and C}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 D-R}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 W), and U}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 S}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 B flash drives.\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s1\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs32\cf2\lang1033 {\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 Output Devices: Monitor\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 The monitor displays information (text and graphics). The resolution and dot pitch determine the quality of the display.\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s1\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs32\cf2\lang1033 {\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 Monitor Resolution and Dot Pitch\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 resolution\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 The }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 screen resolution }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 specifies the number of pixels in horizontal and vertical dimensions of the display device. }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 Pixels}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48  (short for \'93picture elements\'94) are tiny dots that form an image on the screen. A common resolution for a 17-inch screen, for example, is 1,024 pixels wide and 768 pixels high. The resolution can be set manually. The higher the resolution, the sharper and clearer the image is.\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 dot pitch\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 The }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 dot pitch }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 is the amount of space between pixels, measured in millimeters. The smaller the dot pitch, the sharper the display.\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s1\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs32\cf2\lang1033 {\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 Communication Devices\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 A }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 regular modem }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 uses a phone line and can transfer data in a speed up to 56,000 b}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 p}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 s (bits per second). A }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 D}{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 S}{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 L}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36  (digital subscriber line) also uses a phone line and can transfer data in a speed 20 times faster than a regular modem. A }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 cable modem }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 uses the T}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 V cable line maintained by the cable company. A cable modem is as fast as a D}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 S}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 L. Network interface card (}{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 N}{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 I}{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 C}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 ) is a device to connect a computer to a local area network (L}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 A}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 N). The L}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 A}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 N is commonly used in business, universities, and government organizations. A high-speed N}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 I}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 C, called }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 1000BaseT}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 , can transfer data at 1000 m}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 b}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 p}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs36 s (million bits per second).\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s1\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs32\cf2\lang1033 {\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 Programs\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 Computer }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 programs}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 , known as }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 software}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 , are instructions to the computer.\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 You tell a computer what to do through programs. Without programs, a computer is an empty machine. Computers do not understand human languages, so you need to use computer languages to communicate with them.\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 Programs are written using programming languages.\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s1\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs32\cf2\lang1033 {\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 Programming Languages }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs40\cf1 (1 of 3)}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 \par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs44\cf3 Machine Language\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs44 Assembly Language\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs44 High-Level Language\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs44 Machine language is a set of primitive instructions built into every computer. The instructions are in the form of binary code, so you have to enter binary codes for various instructions. Program with native machine language is a tedious process. Moreover the programs are highly difficult to read and modify. For example, to add two numbers, you might write an instruction in binary like this:\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f4\fs44 1101101010011010\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s1\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs32\cf2\lang1033 {\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 Programming Languages }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs40\cf1 (2 of 3)}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 \par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs44 Machine Language\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs44\cf3 Assembly Language\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs44 High-Level Language\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs44 Assembly languages were developed to make programming easy. Since the computer cannot understand assembly language, however, a program called assembler is used to convert assembly language programs into machine code. For example, to add two numbers, you might write an instruction in assembly code like this:\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs44 A}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs44 D}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs44 D}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs2  }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs44 F3 R1, R2, R3\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s1\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs32\cf2\lang1033 {\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 Programming Languages }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs40\cf1 (3 of 3)}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 \par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs44 Machine Language\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs44 Assembly Language\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs44\cf3 High-Level Language\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs44 The high-level languages are English-like and easy to learn and program. For example, the following is a high-level language statement that computes the area of a circle with radius 5:\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s1\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs32\cf2\lang1033 {\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 Popular High-Level Languages\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s1\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs32\cf2\lang1033 {\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 Interpreting/Compiling Source Code\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 A program written in a high-level language is called a }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 source program }{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 or }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 source code}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 . Because a computer cannot understand a source program, a source program must be translated into machine code for execution. The translation can be done using another programming tool called an }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 interpreter}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48  or a }{\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 compiler}{\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 .\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s1\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs32\cf2\lang1033 {\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 Interpreting Source Code\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 An interpreter reads one statement from the source code, translates it to the machine code or virtual machine code, and then executes it right away, as shown in the following figure. Note that a statement from the source code may be translated into several machine instructions.\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s1\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs32\cf2\lang1033 {\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 Compiling Source Code\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s2\li720\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs28\cf2\lang1033\li1 {\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs48 A compiler translates the entire source code into a machine-code file, and the machine-code file is then executed, as shown in the following figure.\par 
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\s1\loch\af1\dbch\f1\fs32\cf2\lang1033 {\b\dbch\af1\loch\f1\fs72\cf1 Operating Systems\par 
